  "measurement_notes": [
    "The full-GPU llama.cpp run is bracketed by XENO RT blocks. The low-VRAM llama.cpp runs sit between the second and third XENO RT blocks. This is A-B-A bracketing, not the specification's preferred ABBA or paired-prompt order.",
    "XENO RT uses a real text prompt and derives decode throughput from seven post-first-token intervals. llama-bench uses synthetic prompt/generation tokens and reports eight timed generation tokens. The comparison is therefore an external diagnostic, not a merge gate.",
    "XENO RT keeps selected expert slots uniformly resident across all 48 layers. llama.cpp -ncmoe moves complete expert tensors for the first N layers to CPU. The approximately matched point compares different offload strategies at similar total device-use readings.",
    "Device memory readings are device-wide observations and include desktop/other-process use. XENO RT additionally reports 8574393616 tracked resident bytes; llama.cpp does not expose an equivalent tracked-allocation field in llama-bench.",
    "The first XENO RT block had a severe measured outlier and a lower median than the two later blocks. Bracketing is retained rather than hiding this drift.",
    "No XENO RT result reaches 100 single-stream decode tokens/s. Hundreds-TPS remains a stretch objective; only the unconstrained llama.cpp full-GPU reference exceeded 100 decode tokens/s here.",
    "This external result does not satisfy the XENO RT auto-admission gate, which requires a same-VRAM comparison against the best viable XENO RT non-hybrid baseline and the prescribed ordering."
  ],
